 ],
 "notes": "I read 48 stations, numbered 115 to 162 in order with no gaps. 28 have an asterisk; 27 of those have a keeper's name and one, Point of Ayre, does not. One unmarked station also names a keeper, so 28 rows have keepers in all.\n\nHow I transcribed it:\n- Fragments: I removed only the indentation at the start of each line. Spacing inside the line is copied exactly as printed. For the two wrapped rows (127 at lines 4658-4659 and 129 at lines 4661-4662), the second fragment is just the continuation text.\n- Numbers: each is printed with a period after it (\"115.\"). I left the period out of the number field. It is still in the fragments.\n- Punctuation: names keep the comma or period that follows them as printed (\"Langness,\", \"Point of Ayre.\"). Keepers keep their final period (\"Robt. Clyne.\"). Strip these if your table needs clean names.\n- Wrapped keepers: \"Messrs Pritchard, Owen, and Hughes.\" (127) and \"Messrs Thomas and Hughes.\" (129) are each one entry, joined across the line break with a single space. \"Messrs Work and Clague.\" (123) is also one entry.\n- Coast: I copied each sub-heading exactly as printed, including its final period: \"Isle of Man.\", \"N. W. England.\", \"North Wales.\", \"Bristol Channel.\", \"South-Western England.\". \"South Wales\" (line 4667) has no period in the print, so it has none here.\n\nOther things to check:\n- 148 Bideford Bar names a keeper, Thomas Gilpin, but has no asterisk. That looks like a missing mark or a keeper who sent no return.\n- 118 Langness and 115 Point of Ayre: these two disagree with the paragraph after the list, which thanks Mr Robert Clyne. Clyne is printed here as \"Robt. Clyne.\" at 117 Langness. Also, Point of Ayre (115) has no asterisk and no keeper.\n- I copied these spellings exactly, but they may be wrong:\n  - \"Air.\" (124) is probably Point of Air (Point of Ayr), Flintshire. It is printed as just \"Air.\"\n  - \"Chickens Rock\" (118) has no apostrophe.\n  - \"Selker\" (121), \"Caldy\" (137) and \"Flatholm\" (142) are period spellings of Selker, Caldey and Flat Holm.\n  - \"Sevenstones\" (154) is printed as one word.\n  - \"Nash (E.)\" (140) means the East (High) Nash light.\n- Lines 4635-4636 have a single column header, \"No. on Map.\". There is no header for the asterisk, type, station or keeper columns.\n- No light characters, heights or other details are printed, so character, place and other are null for every row.",
